## 5. Methodology / Working Process

BuildFlow executes a structured, observable eight-phase workflow upon repository submission:

| Phase | Activity | AI Agent(s) Involved |
|-------|----------|----------------------|
| 1 | Repository ingestion, cloning, and environment setup | System Orchestrator |
| 2 | Goal understanding and requirement extraction | Goal Understanding Agent |
| 3 | Architecture mapping and dependency analysis | Architecture Agent, Tech Stack Agent |
| 4 | RAG indexing of all source files into vector database | RAG Pipeline (ChromaDB / Pinecone) |
| 5 | Bug detection, security audit, runtime error identification | Backend Agent, Security Agent |
| 6 | Performance profiling and optimization | Monitoring Agent, Self-Healing Agent |
| 7 | Feature recommendation and intelligent enhancement | Frontend Agent, Strategy Agent |
| 8 | Code generation, scaffolding, and artifact export | Frontend Code Agent, Backend Code Agent, Scaffolding Agent |

### 5.1 Repository Ingestion
The user submits a GitHub repository URL through the BuildFlow interface. The platform clones the repository into an isolated, sandboxed execution environment and recursively indexes all source files, configuration manifests, and documentation.

### 5.2 RAG Pipeline Construction
All indexed files are chunked, embedded using a high-dimensional vector model, and stored in a vector database (ChromaDB or Pinecone). This RAG index serves as the persistent memory for all downstream agents, enabling semantic search across the full repository during analysis.

### 5.3 Multi-Agent Analysis
Agents execute in coordinated stages, each receiving the shared context enriched by previous agents. The Goal Understanding Agent extracts the project's core intent; the Architecture Agent maps the structural design; the Security Agent performs vulnerability scanning; the Monitoring Agent profiles performance characteristics.

### 5.4 Code Generation & Export
Upon completion of all analysis stages, the code generation agents produce complete, runnable file artifacts — frontend React/TypeScript files, backend FastAPI Python modules, Docker configurations, and CI/CD pipelines. These are written to a structured filesystem and made available for download as a ZIP archive.

